lot."]}, "overview": "Louisiana's 5th District, a Republican-leaning seat in the state's northeast, is open this cycle after Julia Letlow left it to run for the Senate. The district's R+17 partisan lean makes a Democratic takeover a steep climb under any circumstances. State Representative Michael Echols dominates the Republican field financially, with roughly $3 million raised and $1.1 million on hand, while Democrat Dan McKay has raised about $90,000 and reports negative cash. Misti Cordell and several other Republicans trail well behind Echols in funding.\n\nNo public polls or expert ratings cover the race, and prediction markets give Democrats only about a 6 percent chance. Louisiana's jungle primary, with six Republicans and three Democrats on one ballot, could force a runoff, but any Republican would start heavily favored there in this district. The evidence points to a Republican hold.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0238, "margin": -16.55}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0284, "margin": -17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0246, "margin": -17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0227, "margin": -17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0235, "margin": -17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.008, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.019, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0171, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0135, "margin": -17.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0175, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0134, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.0427, "margin": -15.0}]}